module v1.tests.claim.checkpoint_identity_keying_witness_test

import v1.compiler.emit_rust { rust_scalar_checkpoint_render_base }
import v1.compiler.coercion { literal_suffix }
import v1.compiler.artifact { Rust }

data checkpoint_identity_keying_witness_note: String = "EXECUTING evidence for the declaration-identity keying of Rust primitive realization, and for the checkpoint-table bypass that keying does NOT close.\n\nWHY THIS FILE EXISTS RATHER THAN THE ROWS IT REPLACES. The same assertions were authored as generated Rust in v1.compiler.compiler_tests_rust ct_groupcompletion_checkpoint_fires_under_faithful_corpus_test, which emits into src/v1/stage0/src/compiler_tests.rs. That module is declared `#[cfg(test)] mod compiler_tests;` inside the v1-compiler crate, while the only test step CI runs is `cargo test -p v1-compiler-tests`, a SEPARATE package that consumes v1-compiler as an ordinary dependency -- so cfg(test) is never set for it and the module is not compiled, let alone run. Measured by execution rather than by reading: the binary that step builds enumerates 30 tests and none of them is this one, against a positive control confirming the enumeration is non-empty. The assertions were therefore authored, plausible, type-correct and NEVER EXECUTED, and v1.compiler.emit_rust checkpoint_table_bypasses_identity_note nonetheless cited them as the class's executing evidence. That is rung inflation in the disclosure of a ceiling -- the DESIGN section 4b(1) failure applied to the compiler's own self-description, which is worse than sitting low because an inflated class never ranks for climbing. The claim is repaired by making it TRUE here rather than by softening the sentence.\n\nWHAT DISCRIMINATES. rust_scalar_checkpoint_render_base takes a bare type name and the resolved declaration's ident_span file. The first four rows are the keying itself: a name with a table row renders from the table; a name WITHOUT one (Nat) reaches the identity arm and answers differently for two declarations that share a spelling -- dag/std/nat.dag realizes natively, src/v2/std/nat.dag does not, and an EMPTY decl_file (identity unknown at the site) refuses rather than guessing. Those four cannot be satisfied together by any constant, by a disabled mechanism, or by a rule keyed on the name alone, which is precisely the property the deleted global corpus mode lacked.\n\nTHE RESIDUE ROWS ASSERT THE CURRENT BYPASSING ANSWERS ON PURPOSE. The table is keyed on the bare name and consulted BEFORE identity, so a table-present name bypasses declaration keying entirely: Int under a src/v2 path still renders i64 even though that declaration is GroupCompletion<Nat> and not a machine integer. They are counted here rather than assumed closed. They FLIP to Absent when a name gets its own row in v1.compiler.coercion structural_declaration_modules_for, and that flip is the dissolution signal, NOT a regression -- do not make them pass again. Hash already made that flip: lookup_checkpoint (v1.compiler.coercion) now refuses before the bare-name table is consulted whenever decl_file names a module enrolled in structural_declaration_modules_for, and \"src/v2/std/node.dag\" is that module's one enrolled row, so table_present_hash_refuses_under_structural_declaration below is CLOSED rather than residue. String made the same flip in a later change: structural_declaration_modules_for now also enrolls \"String\" => [\"src/v2/std/text.dag\", \"dag/std/string_type.dag\"], both of which declare the structural FreeMonoid<Char> carrier rather than a host string, so table_present_string_refuses_under_structural_declaration_text and its _string_type sibling below are CLOSED and residue_table_present_string_bypasses_identity is retired rather than repaired back to passing. Bool was added in the same change, without ever having a residue row here to flip -- structural_declaration_modules_for now also enrolls \"Bool\" => [\"src/v2/std/logic.dag\", \"dag/std/types.dag\"], both of which declare `type Bool = True | False`, the structural two-variant coproduct rather than a host bool, so table_present_bool_refuses_under_structural_declaration_logic and its _types sibling below are CLOSED from the start; no residue_table_present_bool row ever existed in this file to retire. Int remains open -- it has no row yet -- and is exactly the residue population the next instance of this class inherits.\n\nWHERE THIS EXECUTES, STATED PRECISELY BECAUSE THE PREVIOUS CLAIM'S WHOLE DEFECT WAS AN IMPRECISE ONE. The row is enrolled in gunbc.ci_layer_roots v1_claim_scoped_witness_entries, consumed by v1_claim_scoped_witness_batch, whose source-root envelope is dag plus src/v1 -- the envelope this file needs, since it imports v1.compiler.emit_rust. The entry carries the empty function name, so it expands to every test fn here rather than pinning a roster of them. That batch runs SelectionApplied, so this witness executes on changes that affect it and emits a ScopedWitnessSelectionSkipped receipt with provenance otherwise; it is NOT claimed to run on every PR. That is the correct scope rather than a concession: evidence guarding a decision inside emit_rust should execute when emit_rust changes, and a provenance failure refuses loudly instead of widening. Note also what the enrollment is NOT: witness_discovery_scan_dirs is the owned-data-decl scope, not the witness scope, so membership there would neither add nor remove execution here. The assertions were additionally run directly during authoring -- all fourteen PASS -- but direct execution is evidence the assertions HOLD, never evidence that CI runs them, and conflating those two is the same class of error this file exists to repair.\n\nRE-GROUNDING NOTE (smart-ram-730 blocker, session merry-lark-67): the ORIGINAL String residue row here queried decl_file \"src/v2/std/string.dag\", a path that does not exist anywhere in the corpus -- its predicted flip to `none` was therefore structurally unreachable regardless of whether structural_declaration_modules_for was correct, and the row measured nothing. It is replaced, not repaired, by the two CLOSED rows above keyed on the REAL declarations (src/v2/std/text.dag, dag/std/string_type.dag), each asserting the strong `== none` shape the Hash flip already established rather than the weaker `!= none` the broken row used. Two further specimens close the packet: a positive control (literal_suffix_positive_control_symbol_not_enrolled) proving the gate is not a blanket refusal, since without one every refusal row below would be satisfied vacuously by a mechanism that refuses unconditionally; and literal_suffix -- a DIFFERENT consumer of lookup_checkpoint than rust_scalar_checkpoint_render_base, with its own return shape and its own production call site -- is exercised separately rather than inferred from the type-alias rows, and that direct execution surfaced a genuine, distinct disclosure: the literal-rendering call site in v1.compiler.05_emit emit_literal never threads a real decl_file (LitStr passes the empty string), so literal_suffix at PRODUCTION sites cannot be gated by this roster however correctly the roster and lookup_checkpoint discriminate when a real decl_file is supplied. That is measured directly below, not assumed from the declaration-site gap this note's sibling (checkpoint_table_bypasses_identity_note) already names.\n\ndissolve-on: a corpus-side declaration-to-Rust-type binding supersedes the bare-name row, at which point the residue rows flip and this note's second half retires with them. The extdeps table keeps Rust-type-to-spelling (which is what its cited authority can answer); which dag declaration realizes as which Rust type is a gunbc-corpus fact and does not belong in an upstream row."

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The keying itself. Four answers, one function, three distinct outcomes.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A table-present name renders from the table.
test fn table_present_name_renders_from_the_table() -> Bool {
  rust_scalar_checkpoint_render_base(dag_name: "Int", decl_file: "dag/std/integer.dag")
    == Present { value: "i64" }
}

// A table-ABSENT name reaches the identity arm and realizes natively for the
// std declaration.
test fn table_absent_name_realizes_natively_for_the_std_declaration() -> Bool {
  rust_scalar_checkpoint_render_base(dag_name: "Nat", decl_file: "dag/std/nat.dag")
    == Present { value: "i64" }
}

// THE DISCRIMINATING ROW. Same spelling, different declaring module: the v2
// declaration is the Peano coproduct Zero|Succ and must NOT realize as a machine
// integer. A rule keyed on the name alone cannot pass this and the previous row
// together -- which is exactly what the deleted global corpus mode could not do.
test fn same_spelling_different_declaration_does_not_realize_natively() -> Bool {
  rust_scalar_checkpoint_render_base(dag_name: "Nat", decl_file: "src/v2/std/nat.dag")
    == none
}

// Identity UNKNOWN at the site refuses rather than guessing. The empty decl_file
// is not a third realization, it is the absence of the fact the decision needs.
test fn unknown_declaration_identity_refuses_rather_than_guessing() -> Bool {
  rust_scalar_checkpoint_render_base(dag_name: "Nat", decl_file: "")
    == none
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RESIDUE: the bypass identity keying does not close for every table-present
// name -- only for the ones enrolled in structural_declaration_modules_for
// (v1.compiler.coercion). These assert the CURRENT answers so the remaining
// gap is counted. They flip to `none` when a name gets its own row; that flip
// is the dissolution signal, not a regression.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

test fn residue_table_present_int_bypasses_identity() -> Bool {
  rust_scalar_checkpoint_render_base(dag_name: "Int", decl_file: "src/v2/std/integer.dag")
    == Present { value: "i64" }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CLOSED: Hash, String and Bool are the rows structural_declaration_modules_for
// enrolls (v1.compiler.coercion), so lookup_checkpoint refuses before the
// bare-name table is even consulted. The GUARANTEE this closes for Hash: the
// row targets v1_rt::Hash (`pub type Hash = String` in the seed) while the v2
// declaration is Fnv1a64Structural, a record whose digest field carries a
// `where lower_hex_16` refinement -- realizing that as a bare String alias
// would have rendered away the carrier the construction wall is attached to.
// For String: the checkpoint row targets Rust `String` (plus a
// `.to_string()` literal suffix) while BOTH src/v2/std/text.dag and
// dag/std/string_type.dag declare `type String = FreeMonoid<Char>`, the
// structural free-monoid-over-Char carrier, not a host string -- so a
// reference resolving to either module must refuse the table's native
// spelling rather than silently rendering as Rust String. This is the flip
// this file's note predicted: residue_table_present_string_bypasses_identity
// (previously `!= none`) is replaced by the two CLOSED rows below rather than
// repaired back to a passing bypass -- the flip is the dissolution signal for
// this residue population, not a regression. For Bool: the checkpoint row
// targets Rust `bool` (default_expr "false") while BOTH src/v2/std/logic.dag
// and dag/std/types.dag declare `type Bool = True | False`, the structural
// two-variant coproduct, not a host bool -- so a reference resolving to
// either module must refuse the table's native spelling the same way. Bool
// never had a residue row here to flip; it lands directly as CLOSED.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

test fn table_present_hash_refuses_under_structural_declaration() -> Bool {
  rust_scalar_checkpoint_render_base(dag_name: "Hash", decl_file: "src/v2/std/node.dag")
    == none
}

test fn table_present_string_refuses_under_structural_declaration_text() -> Bool {
  rust_scalar_checkpoint_render_base(dag_name: "String", decl_file: "src/v2/std/text.dag")
    == none
}

test fn table_present_string_refuses_under_structural_declaration_string_type() -> Bool {
  rust_scalar_checkpoint_render_base(dag_name: "String", decl_file: "dag/std/string_type.dag")
    == none
}

test fn table_present_bool_refuses_under_structural_declaration_logic() -> Bool {
  rust_scalar_checkpoint_render_base(dag_name: "Bool", decl_file: "src/v2/std/logic.dag")
    == none
}

test fn table_present_bool_refuses_under_structural_declaration_types() -> Bool {
  rust_scalar_checkpoint_render_base(dag_name: "Bool", decl_file: "dag/std/types.dag")
    == none
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// literal_suffix is a DIFFERENT consumer of the same lookup_checkpoint gate
// (v1.compiler.coercion), not a fact inferable from the rows above. It has its
// own return shape (String? rather than TypeCheckpoint?) and, critically, its
// own call site: v1.compiler.05_emit emit_literal's LitStr arm invokes it as
// `literal_suffix(target: target, dag_name: "String", decl_file: "")` -- a
// string LITERAL has no declaring module to resolve, so that production call
// site can never supply a decl_file the structural roster would match. The
// gate itself still discriminates correctly when a real decl_file IS supplied
// (below), so the roster is not the gap; the gap is that nothing at the
// literal-rendering call site threads one, and this is the honest, disclosed
// shape of that gap rather than an inferred one -- direct execution, not a
// read of the type-alias rows.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Positive control for literal_suffix specifically: a table-present, NOT
// structurally-enrolled name still answers Present. Without this a
// literal_suffix that refused unconditionally would satisfy every refusal
// row below vacuously.
test fn literal_suffix_positive_control_symbol_not_enrolled() -> Bool {
  literal_suffix(target: Rust, dag_name: "Symbol", decl_file: "")
    == Present { value: ".to_string()" }
}

// literal_suffix refuses for String under EITHER real structural declaration,
// exactly like rust_scalar_checkpoint_render_base above -- the same gate, a
// different consumer, its own executed specimen per declaration identity.
test fn literal_suffix_refuses_under_structural_declaration_text() -> Bool {
  literal_suffix(target: Rust, dag_name: "String", decl_file: "src/v2/std/text.dag")
    == none
}

test fn literal_suffix_refuses_under_structural_declaration_string_type() -> Bool {
  literal_suffix(target: Rust, dag_name: "String", decl_file: "dag/std/string_type.dag")
    == none
}

// THE DISCLOSED GAP, EXECUTED RATHER THAN ASSERTED IN PROSE: the real call
// site in emit_literal passes decl_file: "" for every String literal, and an
// empty decl_file can never satisfy decl_file_declares_structurally (it
// returns false before consulting the roster at all -- v1.compiler.coercion
// line ~122). So literal_suffix at PRODUCTION call sites answers exactly as
// it did before this PR, for every String literal, regardless of which
// module the surrounding reference resolves to. This is not a defect this
// row introduces or fixes -- it is a distinct instance of the same class the
// declaration-site `item_text == "String"` bypass names in
// checkpoint_table_bypasses_identity_note (v1.compiler.emit_rust): a call
// site that never threads decl_identity_file/type_reference_decl_file cannot
// be gated by identity however correct the gate itself is. Recorded here so
// literal-position behavior is measured rather than assumed to track
// type-position behavior now that the two are known to diverge in exactly
// this way.
test fn literal_suffix_production_call_site_never_threads_declaration_identity() -> Bool {
  literal_suffix(target: Rust, dag_name: "String", decl_file: "")
    == Present { value: ".to_string()" }
}
